  "notes": "The hardest-country female voice of the 90s, and the entry calls four singles in the 148–172 band \"the strongest fast-train catalogue among the section's female leads\". `bpm` spans the stated 60–172 with `mode` at 161 (\"Halfway Down\" and \"I Try to Think About Elvis\"); the era is the MCA/Epic run, with the 1980s debut excluded exactly as the entry excludes it. Keys are the stated G/A/B♭ of the bluegrass-leaning cuts — the entry names no others, so no fourth key is authored — and the scale weights carry the entry's \"strong modal/mixolydian colour from the mountain repertoire\" — I–♭VII–IV takes joint top weight with I–IV–V, alongside the stated I–IV–I–V frame. Drums have two stated states and the model sits between them: the fast cuts are \"canonical train beat — straight 8th snare stream, sticks, two-beat kick, hat closed on quarters\", while \"the mountain/bluegrass-flavoured cuts move to brushes and drop the kick to 1 only\", which is why `kick.densityPerBar` is [1,3] rather than a flat two-beat. The bluegrass feel rule from the section's grammar block — \"notes are anticipated, in contrast to laid-back blues\" — is `snare.offGridMs` −5, the earliest backbeat in the radio wing here. ⚠ `countermelody.registerOffset` is 7 rather than the roster's usual ±12, and it is the one place in this batch the research demands it: her producer's \"stated harmony habit is mountain-style close intervals — parallel fourths and fifths as well as thirds — so model the harmony stack as fourths/fifths, not the radio thirds\". A fifth is the only interval of that pair this field can spell. ⚠ INFERRED: velocity tiers, the −5 ms offset value itself, `percs` contents and every melody and bassline number. ⚠ Tempo readings [LOW-CONF] as a class; the entry states \"per-track voicings generalised [LOW-CONF]\" and rates itself medium-high.",
